WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:03.570
All right everyone.

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.029
Welcome to the webinar today.

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:13.629
We will be talking about a new functionality
within the E-utilities API that supports accession.version

00:00:13.629 --> 00:00:14.629
identifiers.

00:00:14.629 --> 00:00:21.460
We have materials for this webinar, the PowerPoint
and a few bells and whistles that will be

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:27.519
available for you at the FTP site and you
can get to that through the coursesandwebinars

00:00:27.519 --> 00:00:33.290
link on the screen here and there is a direct
link also, the go.usa.gov URL.

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:39.320
With all of that said, I will start the webinar.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:44.920
What we're talking about today is actually
the fourth webinar in a series of webinars

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:52.100
about changes that have been going on with
the way that NCBI deals with sequence identifiers.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:59.400
Today we are happy to announce that the E-utilities,
our main API for the Entrez system, is now

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:06.560
able to fully support accession.version identifiers,
both as input and output.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:14.509
What I want to do is give you a quick overview
of how that works and point you to some examples.

00:01:14.509 --> 00:01:19.350
As I said we have been doing webinars about
this since the past summer starting back in

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:20.350
July.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:26.409
there are three webinars we put out there
about various topics relevant to the changes

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:31.149
in identifiers for sequence records here at
NCBI.

00:01:31.149 --> 00:01:36.929
Feel free to go back and take a look at those
if you haven't had a chance to view them.

00:01:36.929 --> 00:01:42.420
Very short and sweet, the change that is happening
is that gradually NCBI is phasing out an identifier

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.060
called G.I. number.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:50.729
These are integer identifiers that have been
used for many years for sequences but for

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:56.639
a number of reasons we are phasing those out
and transitioning to a situation where we

00:01:56.639 --> 00:02:04.000
will be using accession.version identifiers
as the primary identifier for sequence records.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.240
Since so many of our tools, including the
E-utilities, have been dependent upon GI numbers

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:16.650
in the past, it was critical for us to be
able to offer these APIs in a form that would

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:24.709
not be dependent upon GIs but would be dependent
upon a different identifier set, accession.version.

00:02:24.709 --> 00:02:33.519
The main thing that we introduced as a parameter
called idtype that will control this.

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:35.890
It works a little bit differently in the various
e-utilities.

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:42.730
I will go through esummary, efetch, esearch,
elink . And then, the final prize package

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:47.841
at the end is how you get sequences that don't
have GIs and what is that about and how you

00:02:47.841 --> 00:02:51.760
can think about that problem going forward.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.450
Let me review quickly again a little bit more
detail about what's changing so everyone is

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:58.390
on the same page.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:00.580
The GI numbers are being phased out.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.709
What this means is they are not being deleted.

00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:07.890
It's important for people to understand we
are not deleting GI numbers but we are not

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:14.250
going to assign GI numbers to a growing number
of newly deposited sequences.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:19.129
If you have a sequence that has a G.I. number
it will continue to have that G.I. number

00:03:19.129 --> 00:03:24.689
and will always be retrievable by that number
for the foreseeable future.

00:03:24.689 --> 00:03:29.799
What's happening now is, as more and more
new studies come in, those will not be assigned

00:03:29.799 --> 00:03:36.150
GI numbers so if you stick with just G.I.
space you won't have access to more and more

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:39.659
of the new data coming in.

00:03:39.659 --> 00:03:44.530
A few things that have already been happening
are that the GI numbers are no longer visible

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:51.689
on the flat file formats on the web or in
FASTA definition lines, including in the BLAST

00:03:51.689 --> 00:03:53.420
databases.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:58.360
So you won't see the GI numbers in those presentations
and again just to be clear those numbers are

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:04.020
still in the raw data files, for example,
the ASN.1 or the XML.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:08.489
They will not be shown in the more public
web friendly presentations.

00:04:08.489 --> 00:04:16.680
After March 15 of this year none of the releases
of GenBank or RefSeq will contain GI numbers

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:22.240
in their presentations in the flat file or
in the FASTA that you get off the ftp site.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.639
That's another thing to be aware of.

00:04:24.639 --> 00:04:27.940
That's what's happening.

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:31.940
Let's talk about E-utilities and what effect
it has on them.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:35.960
For those of you not as familiar I wanted
to go over what the utilities are and what

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:42.590
they do and their function and how they depend
upon the unique identifier.

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:49.370
The E-utilities work in all of the Entrez
databases, from PubMed to the sequence databases,

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:56.169
medical databases, whatever, the e-utilities
function across a wide variety of identifier

00:04:56.169 --> 00:04:57.550
spaces.

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:01.180
What they do is a few functions.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:10.970
ESearch can take a text query and return back
to a set of these unique identifiers, that

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:13.550
correspond to the result set.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:19.370
Those unique identifiers, uids, can be passed
to additional E-utilities to retrieve those

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:20.370
records.

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:26.750
For example, I can pass a UID to ESummary
to get the record summary, or docsum, which

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:32.970
is a short abbreviated metadata about a record
that you commonly see as search results on

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:35.160
the web.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:40.449
Alternatively I can take a UID and pass it
to EFetch which would download the full data

00:05:40.449 --> 00:05:44.240
record in a variety of formats.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.729
PubMed you can get abstract, citation, Medline,
etc., ASN.1, XML.

00:05:48.729 --> 00:05:54.280
For sequences you can get FASTA, flat file,
XML, ASN.1, etc.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:06:00.440
Efetch is a great utility for getting the
data down once you have the identifiers in

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:02.630
hand.

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:07.940
ELink is another powerful utility that lets
you take identifiers from a given database

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:11.170
and find linked records in another database.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:15.639
For example I want to find the PubMed literature
associated with a sequence.

00:06:15.639 --> 00:06:18.759
I can link from the sequence records to PubMed.

00:06:18.759 --> 00:06:24.310
I can link from nucleotide to protein, etc.,
etc.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:28.569
Elink also talks in this UID space.

00:06:28.569 --> 00:06:31.770
One set of UID's in and another out.

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:40.520
Finally, epost is essentially an upload utility
that takes UIDs and puts them onto a temporary

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:44.180
storage facility that we call the Entrez history.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:49.040
And this is a server that stores temporarily
your search results.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:55.990
Then I can take the results from that history
and pass those to fetch or to summary or to

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:59.220
link for further processing.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:03.699
I think it's pretty clear as you look at the
diagram that all of these utilities either

00:07:03.699 --> 00:07:07.810
output UIDs or accept UIDs as inputs.

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:15.110
It's critical to the function of this API
that the UID space be well determined.

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:23.250
For sequence records, talking about nucleotide,
protein, EST and GSS databases, and Popset,

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:29.360
the UID has always been the G.I. number, the
integer identifier.

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:30.720
Not anymore.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:36.490
You can continue doing that but now all of
these utilities will do the same thing for

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:37.490
accession.version.

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:45.510
It's a very powerful upgrade in the functionality
of these APIs, it extends their scope significantly

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:49.629
and we'll talk more about that towards the
end.

00:07:49.629 --> 00:07:55.830
The set of UIDs is now a set of accession.version
identifiers.

00:07:55.830 --> 00:08:00.659
You have the option, it's important to understand
that you can continue to use GI numbers but

00:08:00.659 --> 00:08:04.419
you have the option to use accession.version.

00:08:04.419 --> 00:08:10.840
I listed there the primary databases that
this affects.

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:15.250
Let's step through them and talk about what
would happen.

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:20.930
Luckily ESummary and EFetch get an upgrade
that is silent.

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:26.560
You use the ID parameter as input for the
UID's.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:32.560
So in the first URL showing there, ESummary,
and the database is nuccore, there are two

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:33.630
UIDs there.

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:36.130
That's a traditional ESummary call.

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:42.229
For EFetch it would be the same kind of call,
same UIDs, but I might put rettype equals

00:08:42.229 --> 00:08:46.350
fasta to specify I want the FASTA format.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:52.530
What you can do now is instead of using those
integer identifiers, the gi's, you can put

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:55.089
accession.veresion.

00:08:55.089 --> 00:09:00.540
If you put in accession without a version
you get the most recent version of that accession.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:01.790
That just works.

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:04.700
You don't have to do anything else to your
call.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:07.990
You don't have to add an ID type parameter
or anything else.

00:09:07.990 --> 00:09:09.589
It just works.

00:09:09.589 --> 00:09:12.940
This is great and you can just start using
this

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:18.490
For those of you who've been using these APIs,
you may realize that EFetch has been able

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:20.010
to do this for many years.

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:25.970
It has been able to accept accession.version
for several years along with GIs, it continues

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:27.070
to do that.

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:30.620
In this regard EFetch has not changed.

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:32.320
ESummary has changed.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:38.380
It has never been able to accept accessions
as input but it can now.

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:41.050
That's what's happening with ESummary and
EFetch.

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:42.050
Pretty simple.

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:45.750
You don't have to do anything, you can just
use accessions now.

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:55.620
ESearch is more interesting because what ESearch
can do now is output data as accession.version.

00:09:55.620 --> 00:10:02.350
What ESearch does is takes a text query, like
foxp2[title] AND human[organism].

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:05.230
I want the human sequences for foxp2.

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:08.550
Before, I would assemble this call.

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:13.170
What that would give me is a list of GI numbers
back.

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:19.120
What it does now is you have the option, if
you add the ID type parameter, and give it

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:28.310
the value acc, so &amp;idtype=acc is appended
to that same search call, you will get something

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:29.310
different.

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:31.089
This is what you get different.

00:10:31.089 --> 00:10:39.130
The default without using ID type is a list
of the top 20 GI numbers.

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:42.320
Unique identifiers that match your query.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:43.769
Those should be nucleotide GIs.

00:10:43.769 --> 00:10:50.350
If you use ID type equals acc, you get back
the accession.versions.

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:51.510
This is cool.

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:55.399
You get the accession.version as if the GIs
don't exist.

00:10:55.399 --> 00:11:00.790
You get them back directly and can use them
for further processing.

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:04.180
Elink is going to behave in much the same
way.

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:13.060
It takes sets of identifiers as input and
gives you sets of identifiers as output, in

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:14.880
the same or a different database.

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:20.570
A very simple example is here's a nucleotide
G.I. number and I want to find the protein

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:26.830
that it encodes so I say dbfrom=nuccore, db=proetin,
and there is my ID.

00:11:26.830 --> 00:11:33.440
I would expect to get back the protein G.I.
that is associated with this.

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:40.690
If I use ID type equals acc, append that to
the URL, now it allows me to do two things.

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:44.829
I can have an accession number as input.

00:11:44.829 --> 00:11:51.829
So I don't have to use a G.I. number and I
will get back accessions in the output.

00:11:51.829 --> 00:11:54.960
Here's a comparison of the XML that I would
get back from those two calls.

00:11:54.960 --> 00:12:05.510
If the ID type is not set, the default is
essentially GI numbers.

00:12:05.510 --> 00:12:13.630
Here's my nuccore dbfrom, here is the id in
nuccore, the link is to protein, here is the

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:18.810
ID number for protein, 34577063.

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:23.279
That's the G.I. number of the protein linked
to the incoming nuccore record.

00:12:23.279 --> 00:12:25.329
But if I use ID type equals acc.

00:12:25.329 --> 00:12:33.900
What you see on the right side, is that the
NM_ record, 001126.3, is now linked to NP_001117.2.

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:37.449
so I get the accessions back immediately.

00:12:37.449 --> 00:12:42.829
ID type equals acc is required for ELink and
ESearch if you want to get the accessions

00:12:42.829 --> 00:12:46.209
in the output.

00:12:46.209 --> 00:12:52.260
Before we do a little bit of playing with
this I want to talk about some expanded scope

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:56.620
that the e-utilities now have access to because
of this change.

00:12:56.620 --> 00:13:02.390
It's important to understand this so you don't
run afoul of the way the data are restructured

00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:07.990
now and be aware that these changes will continue
happen as things progress.

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:12.620
we have sequences that don't have GIs.

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:18.160
That's an issue for a number of reasons because
a sequence without a G.I. is not part of the

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:19.710
Entrez system.

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:25.320
It's not part of Entrez Nucleotide, nuccore,
or Entrez Protein or any other Entrez database.

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:28.050
That has several consequences.

00:13:28.050 --> 00:13:30.500
ESearch cannot find them.

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:35.339
They are not indexed in Entrez, so esearch
has no access to them.

00:13:35.339 --> 00:13:40.920
They do not have document summaries, they
are not in Entrez so ESummary cannot retrieve

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:42.250
them.

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:53.899
They have no Entrez links so ELink cannot
perform links between gi-less objects.

00:13:53.899 --> 00:13:57.650
Also they cannot be placed on the Entrez history.

00:13:57.650 --> 00:14:02.690
For example, EPost cannot handle or process
them.

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:09.339
For those of you who are e-utility aficionados,
if you use ESearch with usehistory=y, it won't

00:14:09.339 --> 00:14:15.070
work for a gi-less sequence because they can't
be put on the history, they are not part of

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:16.710
Entrez.

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:21.490
But EFetch can now download them.

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:27.800
EFetch has become an odd man out in that it
now has a special function, that it has access

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:33.280
to the whole world of GI-less sequences through
their accession.version.

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:38.990
All NCBI sequences, whether they have a gi
number or not, have a accession.version identifier

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:42.610
and EFetch now can access all of them.

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:48.180
This is brand-new functionality that we have
never had before, that API access to all of

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:54.320
these GI-less sequences that still have a
accession.version.

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:56.940
So what are we talking about?

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:58.610
A lot of WGS and TSA.

00:14:58.610 --> 00:15:05.480
So whole genome shotgun datasets and transcriptome
shotgun assembly datasets.

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:11.670
Many of these data sets do not have GIs for
their contigs.

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:17.100
They may have a G.I. number for their master
record but they will not in many cases have

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:20.170
GI numbers for all the congtigs.

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:27.040
Ultimately most new sequences, eventually
almost all, will be like this.

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:31.050
They will not have GI numbers but they will
have accession.version.

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:36.060
It's important to understand that this is
the future and where things are moving so

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:40.990
that accession.version becomes the main identifier
that you can depend on.

00:15:40.990 --> 00:15:48.290
A cardinal example currently is the Norway
Spruce project, CBVK.

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:52.760
This is a very large project with over 1 million
contigs.

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:57.680
Because of the size it was not assigned GI
numbers.

00:15:57.680 --> 00:16:02.060
It has one G.I. number that represents the
master record.

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:10.470
So CBVK and all those zeroes, that single
representative record, which has no data in

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:14.410
it except metadata, that one record is indexed
in Entrez.

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:21.600
If you search for Norway Spruce in Nucleotide
you would find that record but all of the

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:30.240
million contigs are not available to you from
entrée and have not been able to be available

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:33.540
to from the API e-utilities.

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:36.580
They are now.

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:42.110
One question that folks might have is how
do I check if a sequence has a G.I. number

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:43.600
or not?

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:51.259
The easy way to do this programmatically is
to use ESummary because it will immediately

00:16:51.259 --> 00:16:57.160
return to you in error tag telling you that
this accession does not have an identifier,

00:16:57.160 --> 00:16:59.070
a gi identifier.

00:16:59.070 --> 00:17:03.800
And you can do that with a mixed bag of input
and esummary will very politely tell you which

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:05.650
ones work and which ones don't.

00:17:05.650 --> 00:17:11.070
At this point that's our recommendation as
to the best way to check.

00:17:11.070 --> 00:17:15.360
Esummary is fast and it can process thousands
of records in a given URL, so it's a good

00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:20.950
way to parse through a big list if you want
to find out what has a G.I. number or not.

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:26.280
There are other approaches if you have massive
projects and you are concerned about using

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:31.390
ESummary, write to us, talk to us, we can
give you some additional help.

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:38.160
What I'm going to do now before I go to this
slide is do some demo.

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:45.970
We will go through a few calls so you can
see how they work.

00:17:45.970 --> 00:17:51.470
Also this little Perl script, an incredibly
simple little script, I've put on the ftp

00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:53.440
site in case any of you want to play with
it.

00:17:53.440 --> 00:17:55.500
I'm just going to explore the calls a little
bit.

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:59.860
I'm going to actually show you what the Perl
script looks like.

00:17:59.860 --> 00:18:02.080
That's it.

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:10.660
All it does is post a URL and I have an array
here of eight sample URLs.

00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:15.770
You can explore this and edit as you want
and play with this functionality.

00:18:15.770 --> 00:18:21.980
The first URL is an ESummary and that will
get back two sequence records specified by

00:18:21.980 --> 00:18:24.190
an accession.

00:18:24.190 --> 00:18:29.310
This is something we haven't been able to
do in the past, so to run this little script,

00:18:29.310 --> 00:18:35.240
I just say perl geturl.pl with index one.

00:18:35.240 --> 00:18:37.860
I review that and I get some data.

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:42.570
What you see here are two docsum records.

00:18:42.570 --> 00:18:44.070
The first one here.

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:46.570
And the nice thing is it works.

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:48.760
In the past this would have given an error.

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:51.810
I get my accession back and get my record
back.

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:56.780
For those of you that are parsing docsums
you might want to be aware we have a new tag

00:18:56.780 --> 00:19:00.270
within the docsums called Accession.version.

00:19:00.270 --> 00:19:03.970
That thing is brand new and it has the accession.version
in it.

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:08.470
That is something you can parse out directly
and that's where the identifier is now.

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:11.440
Of course the GI numbers going to be there
also.

00:19:11.440 --> 00:19:15.520
And again, if it doesn't have a G.I. number,
ESummary can't find it.

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:17.990
That's how esummary's going to work.

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:20.340
Now esearch.

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:29.630
Two ESearch calls, one will look for foxp2[title]
and the other one has human[organism] without

00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:30.630
$idtype=acc.

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:32.520
The other one as $idtype=acc appended.

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:36.080
So let's do the first one.

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:40.840
As I was showing in the slides, this is what
I get back; I get 48 things back.

00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:45.150
Here is the top 20; I get gi numbers.

00:19:45.150 --> 00:19:52.660
All I have to do is add that ID type equals
acc in that URL and i get back the accessions.

00:19:52.660 --> 00:19:55.170
It is quick and simple.

00:19:55.170 --> 00:19:58.310
All right, going on, ELink.

00:19:58.310 --> 00:20:04.720
We can do elink just as we did esearch, linking
from a nuccore record to a protein record.

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:06.700
Here is the id.

00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:12.220
It's also NM_001126, so we will do it both
ways.

00:20:12.220 --> 00:20:18.600
Just know you have to include idtype=acc at
the end, if I'm going to do the accession.

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:24.420
So my first elink call, again, has the G.I.
number as input and gives me the gi number

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:26.400
for the output for the protein.

00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:36.320
If I do idtype=acc, here is the NM_ as the
input, the NP_ is the output.

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:40.780
You can do this if you are linking across
from non-sequence to sequence databases.

00:20:40.780 --> 00:20:45.220
So if I'm going from pubmed to nuccore or
pubmed into protein, you will use idtype=acc.

00:20:45.220 --> 00:20:49.200
And, of course, PubMed will not have an accesion
number.

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:50.590
That's fine.

00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:56.130
You'll use pmid's as your input to ELink but
the output from ELink will be accession numbers

00:20:56.130 --> 00:20:58.550
in protein space.

00:20:58.550 --> 00:21:01.330
That will all work just fine.

00:21:01.330 --> 00:21:02.520
All right.

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:04.011
Let's take a look at the last ones here.

00:21:04.011 --> 00:21:06.140
Here is EFetch.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:09.540
Here is some of that Norway Spruce data.

00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:15.080
This is WGS data that we've not been able
to give you before with EFetch.

00:21:15.080 --> 00:21:21.330
That URL, here is the ID, that long accession,
the accession for the contig.

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:26.320
And I'm going to get FASTA back.

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:27.320
So I do that.

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:34.470
Boom, there is the FASTA for that wgs contig
and if I scroll up to the top, there it is,

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:35.470
Picea abies.

00:21:35.470 --> 00:21:40.300
There is the data, it has a contig name.

00:21:40.300 --> 00:21:43.390
All of this little FASTA definition line is
something we are providing.

00:21:43.390 --> 00:21:47.960
It doesn't exist in Entrez, but we are providing
one to you.

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:51.250
And so here it is, you can get those data.

00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:56.580
It's very likely if you want the entire project,
ftp might be a better method.

00:21:56.580 --> 00:22:02.560
But, this is a way you can get contig by contig
if you want to get a set of contigs down from

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:05.180
one of these sets.

00:22:05.180 --> 00:22:09.270
Last but not least, ESummary as a check.

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:14.470
How do I check to see whether my sequence
has a G.I. number?

00:22:14.470 --> 00:22:17.060
I'm going to try ESummary with the CBVK.

00:22:17.060 --> 00:22:20.310
The same accession of just retrieved with
efetch.

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:22.300
Let me see what happens.

00:22:22.300 --> 00:22:29.030
But I'm going to try it in a mixed list of
other things; an NM_ record and a GenBank

00:22:29.030 --> 00:22:30.030
record.

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:35.670
How would it look if I had a mixed list and
a bomb in their that doesn't have a G.I. number,

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:37.470
what is esummary going to do?

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:43.870
ESummary in the case where I just give it
that Norway Spruce wgs contig, it very politely

00:22:43.870 --> 00:22:46.990
tells me, ERROR Invalid uid at this position.

00:22:46.990 --> 00:22:53.250
I know that is the contig that had no UID.

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:59.180
If I give it the three identifiers, two of
which have GI numbers and one doesn't.

00:22:59.180 --> 00:23:01.310
This is the data I get back.

00:23:01.310 --> 00:23:06.620
At the top you see, ERROR Invalid uid at this
position.

00:23:06.620 --> 00:23:11.140
And then I get the two valid docsums back
as expected.

00:23:11.140 --> 00:23:16.140
So this should be a fairly rapid way to quickly
check a list of data to say what has gi's

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:18.450
and what doesn't.

00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:23.760
This little Perl script, this almost brainlessly
simple Perl script.

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:27.210
It is on the FTP site and you can play with
it if you want.

