00:24:59.640 --> 00:25:00.810
Here's a few others.

00:25:00.810 --> 00:25:05.330
The first one is about downloading WGS sequences
from the FTP site.

00:25:05.330 --> 00:25:10.800
Given a top-level WGS project ID, so that's
essentially that prefix, what's the best way

00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:15.890
to programmatically get the ftp location for
the entire assembly?

00:25:15.890 --> 00:25:22.470
One way you can do that is to use the BioProject
database.

00:25:22.470 --> 00:25:29.040
That has a good place to search for these
data sets.

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:35.830
That has metadata in it that will allow you
to find the project that is the source of

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:37.440
the WGS data.

00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:43.340
You can link from BioProject into nuccore
and that will give you the master record,

00:25:43.340 --> 00:25:46.180
if it's not in the BioProject data itself.

00:25:46.180 --> 00:25:47.620
That's one avenue.

00:25:47.620 --> 00:25:55.600
You then would be able to construct an FTP
URL down to the FTP site to get that data

00:25:55.600 --> 00:25:58.050
from Genbank.

00:25:58.050 --> 00:26:02.970
Another way you could approach that task of
getting the appropriate accession is through

00:26:02.970 --> 00:26:04.700
nuccore itself.

00:26:04.700 --> 00:26:11.080
At this point my understanding is that all
of our WGS projects -- or at least the vast

00:26:11.080 --> 00:26:15.520
majority -- will be assigned a G.I. number
for their master record.

00:26:15.520 --> 00:26:18.560
And that master record would be indexed with
some metadata.

00:26:18.560 --> 00:26:26.010
So if you're trying to search organism or
a title or something like that, you may be

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:27.430
able to find it that way.

00:26:27.430 --> 00:26:31.800
Then you can construct an FTP URL into Genbank.

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:33.170
I hope that helps.

00:26:33.170 --> 00:26:36.710
If you want more detail on that, let us know
and we can provide you with some additional

00:26:36.710 --> 00:26:37.710
suggestions.

00:26:37.710 --> 00:26:45.210
I think the structure of the FTP site is a
formal way of doing that based on those accessions.

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:49.870
What I will do is put that -- I'll have to
look at it so I can describe it properly -- I

00:26:49.870 --> 00:26:54.720
will put that in the Q&amp;A document so everybody
can take a look at that.

00:26:54.720 --> 00:26:59.420
The other question that I see here is can
esummary maintain the order of the input UID

00:26:59.420 --> 00:27:02.710
even if there is a GI-less accession.version
in there?

00:27:02.710 --> 00:27:08.370
That's a good question and one that we're
thinking about ourselves.

00:27:08.370 --> 00:27:15.640
At the moment the order should be maintained
for those records that do have GIs.

00:27:15.640 --> 00:27:20.790
It appears what's happening is we are writing
the errors at the top of the output screen.

00:27:20.790 --> 00:27:25.470
So there's a disruption in the current order
of the input.

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:26.470
That's a good suggestion.

00:27:26.470 --> 00:27:31.770
We will take that up with the development
staff and see if we can do something to address

00:27:31.770 --> 00:27:32.770
that.

00:27:32.770 --> 00:27:36.500
At the moment you're going to be stuck with
finding the errors at the top and the rest

00:27:36.500 --> 00:27:40.400
should be in the order in which you submitted
them.

00:27:40.400 --> 00:27:41.400
